Let pushline have the cells again

0007 made the converted cells optional for a callback that keeps its own
copy of the departing row. That copy is right for a scroll, where a
moverect follows every push and keeps it in step, and wrong for a
resize: shrinking pushes several rows with nothing moved between them,
and reflowing builds rows the caller's copy never held. BeerSSH read its
row 0 once per push there and filled the scrollback with one line
repeated -- the window then showed it a dozen times over when it grew.

Add vterm_screen_callbacks_pushline_with_cells(), the way back from
0007's opt-in, so a caller can have the cells across vterm_set_size and
go without them for ordinary scrolling.

--- a/include/vterm.h
+++ b/include/vterm.h
@@ -622,6 +622,10 @@
 
 int vterm_screen_is_eol(const VTermScreen *screen, VTermPos pos);
 
+/* The way back from vterm_screen_callbacks_pushline_without_cells(): the
+ * pushline callbacks are given their cells again. */
+void vterm_screen_callbacks_pushline_with_cells(VTermScreen *screen);
+
 /**
  * Same as vterm_state_convert_color_to_rgb(), but takes a `screen` instead of a `state`
  * instance.
--- a/src/screen.c
+++ b/src/screen.c
@@ -1131,6 +1131,11 @@
   }
 }
 
+void vterm_screen_callbacks_pushline_with_cells(VTermScreen *screen)
+{
+  screen->pushline_without_cells = false;
+}
